      "theme_id": "moving_forward",
      "units": [
        {
          "unit_id": "moving_in",
          "title": "A Question at the Harbour",
          "description": "Verbos seguidos de -ing o de to: remember, stop, try, regret y otros que cambian de significado. Además, be used to, get used to y look forward to + -ing.",
          "lessons": [
            {
              "lesson_id": "moving_in_lesson",
              "title": "Verb patterns: -ing or to-infinitive",
              "steps": [
                {
                  "phase": "context",
                  "type": "audio_context",
                  "content": {
                    "title": "A question at the harbour",
                    "dialogue": [
                      {
                        "speaker": "Daniel",
                        "text": "Lucía, I've been meaning to ask you something."
                      },
                      {
                        "speaker": "Lucía",
                        "text": "That sounds serious. Go on."
                      },
                      {
                        "speaker": "Daniel",
                        "text": "Would you consider moving in with me? My contract ends in June."
                      },
                      {
                        "speaker": "Lucía",
                        "text": "Oh! I'd love to… but I'd miss living with Priya."
                      },
                      {
                        "speaker": "Daniel",
                        "text": "I remember you saying that the house was the best thing about Bristol."
                      },
                      {
                        "speaker": "Lucía",
                        "text": "It was, at the beginning. But now I can't imagine not seeing you every day."
                      },
                      {
                        "speaker": "Daniel",
                        "text": "So is that a yes?"
                      },
                      {
                        "speaker": "Lucía",
                        "text": "It's a yes. But you'll have to stop leaving your socks everywhere."
                      },
                      {
                        "speaker": "Daniel",
                        "text": "I'll try to be tidier. I promise."
                      },
                      {
                        "speaker": "Lucía",
                        "text": "And I need to tell Priya. I regret not telling her that we were thinking about it."
                      },
                      {
                        "speaker": "Daniel",
                        "text": "She'll be happy for you. She suggested looking for flats together last month, remember?"
                      },
                      {
                        "speaker": "Lucía",
                        "text": "That's true! I'd forgotten about that."
                      },
                      {
                        "speaker": "Daniel",
                        "text": "Shall we stop to get a coffee and start looking online?"
                      }
                    ],
                    "passage": "April. Lucía and Daniel are walking along the harbour on a Sunday afternoon."
                  }
                },
                {
                  "phase": "discovery",
                  "type": "multiple_choice",
                  "content": {
                    "instructions": "Fíjate en el diálogo y responde.",
                    "items": [
                      {
                        "question": "\"Would you consider moving in with me?\" Consider + …",
                        "options": [
                          "-ing",
                          "to + verb"
                        ],
                        "answer": 0
                      },
                      {
                        "question": "\"I remember you saying that…\" Remember + -ing refers to…",
                        "options": [
                          "a memory of the past",
                          "something to do later"
                        ],
                        "answer": 0
                      },
                      {
                        "question": "\"You'll have to stop leaving your socks everywhere.\" means…",
                        "options": [
                          "dejar de dejar los calcetines",
                          "pararse para dejar los calcetines"
                        ],
                        "answer": 0
                      },
                      {
                        "question": "\"Shall we stop to get a coffee?\" means…",
                        "options": [
                          "pararnos a tomar un café",
                          "dejar de tomar café"
                        ],
                        "answer": 0
                      }
                    ]
                  }
                },
                {
                  "phase": "grammar",
                  "type": "grammar_rule",
                  "content": {
                    "tag": "verb_patterns",
                    "title": "Verb patterns: -ing or to-infinitive",
                    "explanation": "Algunos verbos van seguidos de «-ing» («consider», «imagine», «miss», «suggest», «enjoy», «avoid», «mind») y otros de «to» + verbo («agree», «decide», «promise», «need», «hope», «mean»).\n\nUnos pocos cambian de significado según la forma:\n- «remember» / «forget» + «-ing» = recordar / olvidar algo que pasó; + «to» = acordarse / olvidarse de hacer algo\n- «stop» + «-ing» = dejar de; + «to» = pararse para\n- «try» + «to» = intentar; + «-ing» = probar a\n- «regret» + «-ing» = arrepentirse de; + «to» = lamentar (al comunicar algo)",
                    "table": {
                      "headers": [
                        "Verbo",
                        "+ -ing",
                        "+ to + verbo"
                      ],
                      "rows": [
                        [
                          "remember",
                          "recordar algo pasado: I remember you saying that.",
                          "acordarse de hacer algo: Remember to call Priya."
                        ],
                        [
                          "forget",
                          "olvidar algo pasado: I'll never forget meeting you.",
                          "olvidarse de hacer algo: Don't forget to pay the deposit."
                        ],
                        [
                          "stop",
                          "dejar de: Stop leaving your socks everywhere.",
                          "pararse para: Let's stop to get a coffee."
                        ],
                        [
                          "try",
                          "probar: Try opening the window.",
                          "intentar: I'll try to be tidier."
                        ],
                        [
                          "regret",
                          "arrepentirse: I regret not telling her.",
                          "lamentar (formal): We regret to inform you…"
                        ]
                      ]
                    },
                    "examples": [
                      "Would you consider moving in with me?",
                      "I remember you saying that the house was the best thing about Bristol.",
                      "You'll have to stop leaving your socks everywhere.",
                      "Shall we stop to get a coffee?"
                    ]
                  }
                },
                {
                  "phase": "grammar",
                  "type": "grammar_rule",
                  "content": {
                    "tag": "used_to_forms",
                    "title": "be used to, get used to, look forward to + -ing",
                    "explanation": "«Be used to» + «-ing» o nombre: estar acostumbrado a algo:\n- «I'm used to living with Priya.»\n- «She isn't used to the noise.»\n\n«Get used to» + «-ing» o nombre: acostumbrarse (el proceso):\n- «You'll get used to my socks!»\n- «It took me months to get used to the weather.»\n\nNo los confundas con «used to» + verbo (hábito pasado):\n- «I used to live in Madrid.»\n\nEn estas expresiones «to» es una preposición, así que va seguida de «-ing», no del infinitivo (nunca «used to work» con este sentido). Lo mismo pasa con «look forward to» (tener ganas de), «object to» y «be» / «get accustomed to»:\n- «I'm used to working late.»\n- «I'm looking forward to living with you.»",
                    "table": {
                      "headers": [
                        "Estructura",
                        "Ejemplo",
                        "Significado"
                      ],
                      "rows": [
                        [
                          "used to + verbo",
                          "I used to share a flat.",
                          "antes compartía (ya no)"
                        ],
                        [
                          "be used to + -ing",
                          "I'm used to sharing a flat.",
                          "estoy acostumbrada"
                        ],
                        [
                          "get used to + -ing",
                          "I'll get used to living with you.",
                          "me acostumbraré"
                        ],
                        [
                          "look forward to + -ing",
                          "I'm looking forward to living with you.",
                          "tengo ganas de"
                        ]
                      ]
                    },
                    "examples": [
                      "I'm used to living with Priya and Ollie.",
                      "You'll have to get used to my terrible singing.",
                      "Daniel isn't used to sharing a bathroom.",
                      "I'm really looking forward to living together."
                    ]
                  }
                },
